From the P?S8 Google group

---------- Forwarded message ---------
From: cjl <Unknown>
Date: Tuesday, June 5, 2018 at 12:11:24 AM UTC-5
Subject: Documenting/fleshing out some obscure weaknesses of the basic 
P?S/8 system [and prep for the SHELL]
To: P?S/8 <Unknown>


There are a lot of loose ends to be dealt with before it is viable to 
release the next clean release.  Most are so it is possible for a clean 
usage of the SHELL to eliminate some device-specific kludges, and some old 
minor issues for which it is time to unify things as intended.

I am also adding the unterpinnings so the SHELL will be able to execute 
certain programs from the present [known as "basic" P?S/8 system.  Some 
programs do not serve to be made available more than once, and each system 
is perfectly adequate for these specifics.  As such, I have to implement 
the rational way to have these available to the SHELL sooner rather than 
later.  It's a good time also because of the need to get rid of some more 
serious kludges which involve some complex interactions, and all affected 
will be fixed at once.  [I know, no details, but it's more important that 
everyone know I am dealing with them, and they take time, and little is 
gained me taking about the details as opposed to implementing the improved 
version.  On a longer-term lower-priority basis, I may show how the before 
became the after, but it's more important it be done than talking about it

Additionally, for those who are not seasoned users, it is suffice to just 
learn the current method, and not the "bad old days" etc.

So, if you want something gory, I can provide some details, otherwise, best 
I make them happen first, etc.

Some of these changes are a "set" in that you have to remove all of them so 
that they all can start being changed to a new compatibility level, as well 
as fixing the problem.  Thus, I will have the dilemma of testing components 
without "partners" by whatever means seems fit in the short-term, until all 
relevant changes are done.

This is more a matter of a lot of little annoyances, as opposed to a 
perceived rewrite.  however, many system commands will be used in the same 
manner,yet the "innards" will be changed, "invisible" to the user for the 
most part.

Some will make it easier to create the system itself [mostly my problem] 
and will be some preliminary steps towards making others be able to do it 
with my guidance, but without personally me, etc.

Also, this wull be the last nail in the coffin if requiring any part of 
OS.8 to finish the process, although in some cases, new utilities will 
appear that have a side benefit to OS/8 itself, helping to fix bugs in that 
system [only to a limite extent; the mission is not to merely fix OS8, but 
to eventually obsolete it].


As the pieces start falling into place, I will from time to time announce 
them here; be advised that the improvement may be temporarily incompatible 
with the eventual overall improvement due to the nagging dependencies.

Also, part of the development is being done by maintaining the source files 
in Windows and not OS/8 which has so many limitations already overcome in 
P?S/8.

I will give some details on only one issue:

OS/8 date handling basically died in 1977, but through some extremely ugly 
kludges, appears to some to be viable until 2001, now itself in the past.  
Unfortunately, this conventional wisdom is just plain untrue, but the 
original game plan was to bamboozle some users, so don't blame yourself if 
you also got fooled.  Many thousands went that route before you.  [I can 
definitely prove it's bogus; but this is just an inconvenience, and not 
really important].

In contrast with this, P?S/8 implemented a totally different form of kludge 
to better support the date.  It involves having to make a few patches to 
the system [and keeping records of where to apply the few patches] and this 
must be done every 8 years ;but there is a 3+year grace period!].

Also, besides the nuisance, the year range for which this applies starts in 
1960 and ends in 2099  But the patches must be done every 8 years. The 
current P?S.8 has been patches so the oldest expressible date is 
01-Jan-2016, and it can be used without patching until around 2027.  A new 
release will be forthcoming before that time; just wait for the new 
reelease.  the kludge and responsibility will be lifted and the date range 
settable by ordinary means will increase to 91-Jan-1900 through 
31-Dec-2411.  [Note: This may sound excessive, but the history of many DEC 
and other computers is that once motivated, and effort expended, no one 
wants to hear about the problem ever again.  Unfortunately, for OS/8 that 
was 1978.  But OS/8 is a mediocre system that more hatched than was 
planned.  Thus, no one around now is gonna have and misgivings about the 
replacement code, etc.

Unfortunately, some system device handlers used locations that must now 
remain reserved; thus some code has to be rewritten until all existing 
handlers are fully updated.  this even slightly affects the TC01/TC08 
version [but it not expected to be hard to fix].  All of this set of 
changes will allow the now fully 18-bit date words to be properly saved 
onto the system device.  [Note: OS/8 does not know how to do this, and 
there are some serious consequences to having an unset date, easily 
forgotten. It causes erroneous dates on files, etc.  This was actually 
fixed in OS/278 V2, but that is only used on DECmate II, III and III+ 
models and is extremely broken in other ways, etc.]

P?S/8 retains the date the last time you set it, so it can't be all that 
far off in general, etc.  But P?S/8 had this feature in 1972 while OS/278 
V2 was released in 1983 [and not retrofit able to earlier OS/8-type 
releases].

cjl

